Root cause:
The bug is caused by the incorrect handling of the end condition in the macros that iterate over the dynamic memory blocks (LMBs). Specifically, when the `drmem_info->lmbs` is NULL, the loop would attempt to access memory beyond the allocated range, leading to a NULL pointer dereference and subsequent kernel crash when manipulating DLPAR parameters. The original loop condition allowed for an out-of-bounds access, which was not properly checked.

Code statements information:
The code statements that lead to the bug are:
```c
for_each_drmem_lmb(lmb) {
    ...
}
```
```c
end = &start[n_lmbs - 1];
```
```c
last_lmb = &drmem_info->lmbs[drmem_info->n_lmbs - 1];
```
Reason:
The bug occurs because the `for_each_drmem_lmb` macro does not properly check if `drmem_info->lmbs` is NULL, allowing the loop to iterate through an invalid memory range. This results in a NULL pointer dereference when accessing `lmb->drc_index` or when calculating the `end` pointer, leading to a crash when manipulating DLPAR parameters.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
```c
end = &start[n_lmbs];
```
```c
limit = &drmem_info->lmbs[drmem_info->n_lmbs];
```
Reason:
The fix modifies the calculation of the `end` pointer to ensure it points to one past the last valid LMB, aligning with common C semantics. Additionally, it introduces a `limit` pointer to check against the actual bounds of the `drmem_info->lmbs` array, preventing out-of-bounds access and thus avoiding the NULL pointer dereference.


